course: everyday
lesson: 303
title: Console games, building, crafts, music and ball games
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many hobbies have a few simple rules that keep you and others safe and make you better at them.
explain: In a motion game on a console you swing your arms, so you need clear space and the wrist strap on, or the controller can fly into the TV or a person.
explain: When you hammer a nail with a parent, hold the nail low near the wood and start with light taps; when the nail stands up by itself, move your fingers away.
explain: Example: a boy in Toronto learning the piano keeps stumbling on one hard bar. He plays that bar slowly ten times until it is right, and only then plays the whole piece faster.
explain: Playing a little every day trains your fingers and brain better than one long session a week, and practising mistakes fast only makes them a habit.
fact: Before a motion game, clear space around you and wear the wrist strap, so you do not hit furniture or people or let the controller fly.
fact: Turns on a shared console should be fair; use an agreed timer and ask an adult if a sibling will not stop.
fact: When hammering, hold the nail low, start with light taps and keep your fingers away once the nail stands.
fact: Wet sand holds its shape better than dry sand, which is why sandcastles are made of damp sand.
fact: Never throw sand, because it hurts and can scratch the eyes.
fact: Ask before using a cardboard box for a model, and recycle it afterwards to save resources.
fact: Keep clay moist under plastic so it does not dry out, and wash your hands after using it.
fact: A kiln for firing clay is extremely hot and is for adults only.
fact: Watercolour and acrylic brushes can be rinsed in water; oil paint needs an adult and a special cleaner, some of which are poisonous.
fact: Practising a musical instrument a little every day builds skill faster than a lot once a week.
fact: Fix a hard bar by playing it slowly first; playing mistakes fast makes them a habit.
fact: When a ball flies at your face, put your hands up and turn your head to protect your nose, eyes and teeth.
fact: Keep a safe distance from anyone swinging a racket or bat, because it can hit your face.
exercise: A girl in Arizona is about to play a dancing game on a console in the living room. What should she do first? | Clear space around her and put on the wrist strap | Stand as close to the TV as she can so she can see the steps clearly | Hold the controller loosely so it moves freely | Turn the volume up so she can hear the music
exercise: Why do motion game controllers have a wrist strap? | So the controller cannot fly out of your hand and hit something | So you can carry it more easily around the house | So it charges faster while you play | So the console knows which player you are
exercise: It is your turn on the shared console, but your brother will not stop playing. What is the best thing to do? | Use the agreed timer and ask an adult if he still refuses | Pull the plug out of the wall to stop him | Grab the controller out of his hands | Wait silently until he finally gets bored and gives you the controller
exercise: You are hammering a nail with your dad. How should you start? | Hold the nail low and give it light taps | Hold the nail right at the top and hit it hard | Hold the hammer near the head and swing fast | Ask your dad to hold the nail while you swing hard
exercise: Why should you keep your fingers away once the nail stands by itself? | If you miss the nail, the hammer will not hit your fingers | The nail always goes in straighter without fingers | Your fingers make the nail rusty | The hammer only works when nothing touches the nail
exercise: Why is it easier to build a sandcastle with damp sand? | Wet sand sticks together and holds its shape | Dry sand is far too heavy to lift into a bucket and carry about | Wet sand is warmer for your hands | Dry sand always blows straight into the sea
exercise: A boy at the beach in Cornwall throws a handful of sand at his friend for fun. Why is this wrong? | Sand in the eyes hurts and can scratch them | Sand is needed to build the castles | The sand will get the towels dirty | Sand is too heavy and might knock him right over into the water
exercise: You want to turn a big cardboard box into a car. What should you do? | Ask if anyone needs the box first, and recycle it afterwards | Take any box you find and throw it in the bin after | Use it only if it has never been opened | Burn it in the garden when you have finished
exercise: You are making a clay pot over two days. How do you stop it drying out overnight? | Cover it with plastic to keep it moist | Leave it on a sunny windowsill overnight | Put it in the oven on low heat | Wrap it in dry paper towels
exercise: Who should use the kiln that fires clay pots at school? | Only the adults, because it is extremely hot | Any pupil who wears oven gloves | Older pupils if a friend watches | Anyone at all, because it switches itself off when it gets too hot
exercise: You have finished painting with oil paints. How should the brushes be cleaned? | An adult cleans them with a special cleaner | You rinse them quickly under the kitchen tap | You leave them in a glass of water overnight | You wipe them on your clothes and put them away
exercise: A girl starts learning the violin. Which practice plan helps most? | Fifteen minutes every day | Two long hours every Saturday | Only when there is a lesson the next day | Whenever she feels like it, about once a month
exercise: A boy keeps making the same mistake in one bar of a piano piece. What should he do? | Play that bar slowly until it is right, then speed up | Play the whole piece much faster to rush past it | Skip that bar each time he plays | Play it loudly so the mistake is hidden
exercise: A ball flies straight towards your face in the playground. What should you do? | Put your hands up and turn your head | Close your eyes tight and stand very still | Open your mouth and shout | Lean forward towards the ball
exercise: Your friend is practising tennis serves. Where should you stand? | Well away from the swinging racket | Right behind her, close enough to watch her swing | Just beside her to pass the balls | In front of her to catch the ball
test: A family in Colorado plays a bowling game on the console, and the youngest stands right next to his sister as she swings. What is the danger? | She could hit him with her arm or the controller | The console might stop counting the score | The TV picture might freeze | Her character might bowl into the gutter
test: Which is true about practising a musical instrument? | Short daily practice builds skill faster than one long weekly session | Practising once a week for a long time is just as good as every day | Playing a piece fast is the quickest way to learn it | Mistakes disappear by themselves if you keep going
test: Why can playing a piece fast with mistakes in it be a problem? | Your fingers learn the mistakes as a habit | It wears out the instrument more quickly | Fast music is always too loud for the neighbours | Teachers can only hear slow playing
test: A girl in Perth rinses her acrylic brush in a jar of water when she has finished. Is this right? | Yes, acrylic and watercolour brushes can be cleaned with water | No, acrylic always needs a strong chemical cleaner, like oil paint does | No, brushes should never get wet at all | No, she must throw the brush away after use
test: Two children at a cricket practice stand right next to a boy swinging a bat. What should they do? | Move further away from the swinging bat | Stand closer so they can catch the ball | Keep watching from where they are | Lie down so the bat goes over them
test: A boy hammers while holding the nail right next to the head of the nail. What could happen? | He could miss and hit his fingers | The nail will go in more crookedly | The hammer handle could break in two | The wood will split along the grain
test: What does a timer do for two siblings sharing a console? | It makes the turns fair, so nobody needs to argue | It makes the games load faster | It lets both play at the same time | It stops the console from overheating when it's played for a long time
test: A pottery class in Wellington leaves the clay uncovered on the table for a week. What will happen? | The clay will dry out and become hard | The clay will turn soft and runny like paint | The clay will grow bigger | Nothing will change at all
test: You find a big box in the garage and want to make a robot costume. What is the right first step? | Ask whether anyone needs the box | Cut it up straight away | Wait until it is dark to take it | Paint it before telling anyone
test: playing the tennis game on the switch, livingrooms full of stuff | Clear space and wear the wrist strap first | Just swing smaller and hope you don't hit anything | Hold the controller loosely | Play standing on the sofa
test: my mate says dry sand makes better castles, right? | No, damp sand holds its shape better | Yes, dry sand packs tighter and stays up longer | Yes, wet sand just falls apart | Only if you add pebbles
test: wanna learn piano fast, practise 3 hrs on sunday only? | A little every day builds skill faster | Yes, one long go is best for learning fast | Yes, but only on weekends | Practise only before a concert
test: ball coming right at my face in dodgeball!! | Hands up and turn your head | Close your eyes and stand still | Open your mouth wide to catch it | Lean forward to meet the ball
explain: A boy in Texas played a sword game on his console without clearing space; he swung his arm into the TV and cracked it, and cut his hand.
explain: A girl in England threw sand at a friend in the sandpit; it went in his eye and scratched it, and he needed eye drops for days.
fact: Sand and grit in the eye can scratch it, which hurts and can need a doctor.
fact: If sand gets in your eye, do not rub it; rinse it with clean water and tell an adult.
fact: A hammer that misses the nail often hits the finger holding it, which is why you start with light taps.
test: sand in my eye after someone chucked it | Don't rub; rinse with clean water and tell an adult | Rub it hard | Blink and keep playing | Use a tissue to pick it out of your eye
test: gonna play the sword game in my little room next to the lamp | Clear space and wear the strap first | It's fine if you swing slowly and carefully | Stand on the bed | Hold the lamp with your other hand
test: hammering, holding the nail right at the top | Hold it low and start with light taps | Hit hard to get it over with quickly | Hold it with your teeth | Close your eyes
test: did u hear a kid threw sand at the beach and "his mate's eye got scratched, he had to wear a patch for weeks". lesson? | Never throw sand; it can scratch the eyes | Sand is soft so it can't hurt anyone's eyes | Only wet sand is dangerous | It's fine if you aim low
test: my brother says a girl swung her controller and "smashed the TV and her dad's nose". what's the true bit | Clear space and wear the wrist strap before motion games | Motion games are always harmless, controllers never fly off | Controllers can't fly off | TVs are too strong to break
test: kids say the boy in band "plays like a cat being sat on". pass it on? | No, that's unkind; practice a little each day helps anyone | Yes, it's funny and true | Yes, and copy his playing at break so everyone laughs | Yes, and tell the teacher
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct technique when commencing to hammer a nail? | The nail is held low and struck with light taps at first | The nail is held at the head and struck hard | The fingers remain upon the nail throughout | The hammer is swung at full force from the very outset so as to save time
exam: Identify the principal reason for which damp sand is preferred to dry sand for building sandcastles. | Wet sand holds its shape better | Dry sand is much heavier to carry | Wet sand is much warmer to touch | Dry sand is prohibited on beaches
exam: It is given that a difficult bar of music is to be mastered. Select the response consistent with sound practice. | It shall be played slowly first | It shall be played quickly until correct | It shall be skipped entirely each time | It shall be practised once a week only
exam: State the appropriate response when a ball flies towards one's face. | Raising the hands and turning the head | Closing the eyes tightly and standing completely still | Opening the mouth to shout | Leaning forward towards the ball
